---
import Layout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import JsonLd from '~/components/common/JsonLd.astro';

const metadata = {
  title: 'PVC vs Hypalon Inflatable Boat — Which Material Is Better?',
  description:
    'Compare PVC and Hypalon CSM inflatable boat fabrics across durability, UV resistance, cost, repairability, and lifespan. Find the right material for your boat.',
  ignoreTitleTemplate: true,
};
---

<Layout metadata={metadata}>
  <JsonLd
    type="Article"
    article={{
      headline: 'PVC vs Hypalon Inflatable Boat — Which Material Is Better?',
      description:
        'Compare PVC and Hypalon CSM inflatable boat fabrics across durability, UV resistance, cost, repairability, and lifespan.',
      datePublished: '2026-07-07',
    }}
    data={{ url: 'https://aquafarer.com/compare/pvc-vs-hypalon' }}
  />
  <JsonLd
    type="BreadcrumbList"
    breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Home', href: '/' }, { name: 'Compare', href: '/compare' }, { name: 'PVC vs Hypalon' }]}
  />
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto py-24 px-6">
    <a href="/products" class="text-sm text-sky-600 hover:text-sky-700">&larr; Products</a>
    <h1 class="mt-4 text-4xl font-semibold text-gray-900">PVC vs Hypalon Inflatable Boat Material</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-gray-500">
      The definitive comparison between PVC (polyvinyl chloride) and Hypalon CSM (chlorosulfonated polyethylene) — the
      two dominant materials used in inflatable boat construction worldwide.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-12 grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-8 border border-gray-200">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900">PVC Fabric</h2>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-sm text-gray-600">
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-sky-500 mt-2 flex-shrink-0"></span><span
              ><strong>Cost:</strong> 30-50% cheaper than Hypalon. Most affordable inflatable boat material on the market.</span
            >
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-sky-500 mt-2 flex-shrink-0"></span><span
              ><strong>Weight:</strong> Lighter than Hypalon. Easier to carry, pack, and transport when deflated.</span
            >
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-sky-500 mt-2 flex-shrink-0"></span><span
              ><strong>Repairability:</strong> Excellent. PVC patches bond easily with standard PVC glue or solvent welding.</span
            >
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-sky-500 mt-2 flex-shrink-0"></span><span
              ><strong>Weld Type:</strong> High-frequency (RF) welding creates strong, consistent seams. Fusion bonding available.</span
            >
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-sky-500 mt-2 flex-shrink-0"></span><span
              ><strong>Best for:</strong> Recreational boats, inflatable SUPs, kayaks, budget-friendly dinghies, seasonal
              use.</span
            >
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-8 border border-gray-200">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Hypalon CSM Rubber</h2>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-sm text-gray-600">
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-emerald-500 mt-2 flex-shrink-0"></span><span
              ><strong>Cost:</strong> Premium material. Significantly more expensive than PVC across all boat sizes.</span
            >
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-emerald-500 mt-2 flex-shrink-0"></span><span
              ><strong>Weight:</strong> Heavier than PVC. Less portable but more robust in demanding conditions.</span
            >
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-emerald-500 mt-2 flex-shrink-0"></span><span
              ><strong>Repairability:</strong> Moderate. Requires two-part adhesive (glue). Repair patches need careful preparation.</span
            >
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-emerald-500 mt-2 flex-shrink-0"></span><span
              ><strong>Construction:</strong> Hand-glued seams or chemical bonding. No RF welding possible with Hypalon.</span
            >
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-emerald-500 mt-2 flex-shrink-0"></span><span
              ><strong>Best for:</strong> Professional RIBs, commercial use, search and rescue, tropical climates, long-term
              fleet ownership.</span
            >
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <section class="mt-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Side-by-Side Comparison</h2>
      <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b-2 border-gray-200">
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-semibold text-gray-700">Factor</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-center font-semibold text-sky-700">PVC</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-center font-semibold text-emerald-700">Hypalon CSM</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              [
                ['Relative Cost', '$ (budget-friendly)', '$$$ (premium)'],
                ['Weight (3m boat)', '25-35 kg', '35-50 kg'],
                ['UV Resistance', 'Good (with UV coating)', 'Excellent (inherent)'],
                ['Chemical Resistance', 'Good', 'Excellent (oil, fuel, acid)'],
                ['Abrasion Resistance', 'Moderate', 'Excellent'],
                ['Flexibility (cold)', 'Stiffens below 0°C', 'Flexible to -20°C'],
                ['Lifespan (typical)', '8-12 years', '15-20+ years'],
                ['Color Retention', 'Fades over time', 'Excellent long-term'],
                ['Seam Strength', 'RF weld = very strong', 'Glued = good'],
                ['Ease of Repair', 'Easy (PVC glue)', 'Moderate (2-part adhesive)'],
                ['Environmental Temp', '-10°C to 50°C', '-20°C to 70°C'],
              ].map((row, i) => (
                <tr class={i % 2 === 0 ? 'bg-white' : 'bg-gray-50'}>
                  {row.map((cell, j) => (
                    <td
                      class={`px-4 py-3 border-b border-gray-200 ${j === 0 ? 'font-medium text-gray-700' : 'text-center text-gray-600'}`}
                    >
                      {cell}
                    </td>
                  ))}
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-16 bg-gradient-to-br from-sky-50 to-blue-50 rounded-2xl p-8 border border-sky-200">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Which Should You Choose?</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-gray-600 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        Choose <strong>PVC</strong> if you are a recreational boater on a budget, need a lightweight portable boat, or use
        your boat seasonally in moderate climates. Modern military-grade PVC (1100-1670 dtex) offers excellent durability
        for most users at half the cost of Hypalon. Choose <strong>Hypalon</strong> if you leave your boat inflated full-time
        in tropical sun, operate a commercial or rescue fleet, need chemical/oil resistance, or plan to keep the boat for
        15+ years. Hypalon's superior UV resistance and longevity justify the premium for professional users.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-6 flex gap-4 flex-wrap">
        <a
          href="/products"
          class="px-6 py-3 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-full text-sm font-medium hover:bg-gray-800 transition"
          >Browse Products</a
        >
        <a
          href="/compare/pvc-vs-hypalon"
          class="px-6 py-3 border border-gray-200 text-gray-700 rounded-full text-sm font-medium hover:border-gray-400 transition"
          >In-Depth Material Guide</a
        >
      </div>
    </section>
  </section>

  <section class="sr-only" data-ai-block="definition" aria-hidden="true">
    <p>
      Comprehensive comparison between PVC and Hypalon CSM materials used in inflatable boat and RIB construction.
      Covers cost, weight, UV resistance, chemical resistance, abrasion resistance, cold flexibility, lifespan, color
      retention, seam strength, repairability, and temperature tolerance. PVC offers 30-50% lower cost, lighter weight,
      RF-welded seams, easy repair with PVC glue, and 8-12 year lifespan with UV coating. Hypalon offers superior UV
      resistance, chemical/oil resistance, cold flexibility to -20°C, 15-20+ year lifespan, excellent color retention,
      but requires glued seams and two-part adhesive for repairs. Recommendation: PVC for recreational/seasonal use on a
      budget; Hypalon for professional/commercial use in UV-intensive environments requiring maximum longevity.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>
